  "accuracy_finding": "THE PARALLEL SCAN IS MORE ACCURATE THAN THE SEQUENTIAL ONE, and the gap WIDENS with n. At 2^24 numpy's sequential f32 cumsum is off by 6.901e-05 relative while the GPU scan is off by 6.732e-07 -- 103x better -- because sequential summation accumulates O(n) rounding error and a block/tree structure accumulates O(log n). The error gap tracks n exactly as that predicts: the two are equal at n=7 and separate by two orders of magnitude by 2^24. This inverts the usual intuition that the GPU answer is the approximate one. MLX's cumsum is ALSO a parallel scan and is accordingly in the same class (9.260e-07 at 2^24, within 1.4x of ours) -- the contrast that matters here is parallel vs sequential, NOT ours vs MLX, and it is not claimed as one.",
  "141_answer": "Apple's simd_prefix_inclusive_sum is worth 1.13x over the textbook threadgroup-memory Hillis-Steele shape at 2^24 (clean arms) and nothing distinguishable at 2^20. Real but modest, and it COSTS max support: Metal has prefix sum and product but no prefix max, so hillis_steele is kept as the only strategy that scans a general associative op."
 },
